1. Shipment ID normalization
Regex used:
str_replace_all("[^A-Z0-9]", "")
Removes everything except letters and numbers.
Optimized regex:
str_replace("^([A-Z]{2,3})([0-9])", "\\1-\\2")
What it does:
Detects any 2–3 letter prefix (FRJ, GT, OS, HK, AA, TR, etc.)
Inserts a dash before the numeric part
Works for all future prefixes without adding new rules
Replaces 6–7 separate str_replace() calls with one generalized rule
Why it’s efficient:
Runs once over the entire column (vectorized), not per row.

2. Weight normalization
Regex used:
str_replace_all("[^0-9.-]", "")
What it does:
Extracts numeric values from messy strings like:
approx 200kg
198kg
150kg
-20
20kg
Why it’s efficient:
One regex handles all noise patterns.

3. Currency normalization
Regex used:
str_replace_all("[A-Z]|€", "")
str_replace_all("[^0-9.-]", "")
What it does:
Removes:
SEK, DKK, NOK, EUR
€ symbol
commas
spaces
trailing punctuation
Then converts to numeric.
Why it’s efficient:
Two regex sweeps clean all currency formats.

4. Delay reason normalization
Regex used:
str_detect(..., "jam")
str_detect(..., "warehouse")
What it does:
Maps messy text like:
Jam
delayed by traffic
warehouse issue
into standardized categories:
traffic jam
warehouse delay
none
Why it’s efficient:
One semantic rule per category, not per row.

5. Flags
No regex here — just logical checks.
